INT. MINIATURE GOLF COURSE - WINDMILL HOLE - DAY

A garish plastic windmill, a fiberglass dinosaur, a pond of dyed-blue water. Sprinklers tick. A line of tourists waits at the tee.

WILLOUGHBY "WILLY" PRATT, 60s, in a golf visor and a pastel polo, grips his putter like a scepter. His wife, PHILIPPA PRATT, 60s, pats her hair and checks her scorecard. Their son, CHAD PRATT, 30s, in a collared shirt with the logo of his own startup, scrolls his phone. Their daughter, PORTIA PRATT, 20s, kicks a tee. And behind them, the hole's reluctant attendant, MR. DOBBS, 50s, sits in a plastic chair under a beach umbrella, reading a paperback with grim concentration.

WILLY
(declaring)
The windmill hole. Where families are made or destroyed.

PHILIPPA
Please don't be dramatic.

WILLY
This is the hole that cost me my first marriage.

PHILIPPA
I'm your first marriage.

WILLY
That is why it hurt.

Chad snorts without looking up. Portia sets her ball on the mat with exaggerated care, takes a stance, and swings. The ball rolls straight, hits the windmill's spinning blade with a hollow clack, and rebounds. It rolls back past her feet, past Chad, past Willy's shoes, and into the blue pond with a plop.

PORTIA
That's cheating.

MR. DOBBS
(not looking up)
It's a windmill.

WILLY
Cheating how?

PORTIA
The blade moves.

MR. DOBBS
That's the idea.

Willy hands her another ball. Philippa lines up her shot with her pinkie extended. Chad finally pockets his phone, steps up with a stiff practice swing that sends a clod of turf across the course and onto Mr. Dobbs's paperback, and then, with no trace of embarrassment, shrugs and tees up his own ball. While Willy lectures Portia about wrist angles, Philippa's ball rolls untouched past the windmill, down a slope, and settles at the lip of the hole.

PHILIPPA
Oh.

Everyone looks at the ball. Willy forgets the lecture. Chad drops his putter. Portia gasps. Mr. Dobbs lowers the paperback, revealing a face that has seen this before.

WILLY
Hole in one?

MR. DOBBS
Not yet.

The windmill blade swings around, taps the ball, and sinks it. A bell dings. A tinny fanfare plays from a hidden speaker. The family goes silent.

MR. DOBBS (CONT'D)
(deadpan)
Congratulations, ma'am. You win a free game.

PHILIPPA
(delighted)
I do?

WILLY
(horrified)
She does?

Philippa throws her arms up. Chad applauds without enthusiasm. Portia stalks to the pond and begins fishing for her ball with her putter. Willy sinks onto the dinosaur's tail, defeated.

WILLY (CONT'D)
In forty years, I have never won a free game.

PHILIPPA
Darling, you have a free game every time you married me.

Mr. Dobbs flips a page of his paperback. Overhead, a plastic seagull swings from a pole, creaking in the wind. Everyone, finally, laughs, even Willy, even Dobbs, though he hides it behind the book.

WILLY
(rising, brushing his shorts)
Next hole. The one with the volcano.

PHILIPPA
Oh, no.

CHAD
Dad, the volcano is a lie.

WILLY
It is a beautiful lie.

PORTIA
(from the pond, lifting a dripping ball)
I got it!

She holds it up like a trophy. Water streams down her arm. Willy salutes. The family troops toward the volcano, still bickering, while Mr. Dobbs returns to his paperback and the sprinklers tick on.
